", \"has_fraudulent_dispute\": \"False\", \"is_refused_by_adyen\": \"False\", \"aci\": \"F\", \"acquirer_country\": \"NL\"}\n\nUse the execute_python tool to load, explore and analyse the data. Variables persist between calls; pandas is preloaded as `pd`. Read manual.md and payments-readme.md first where the question depends on domain terms (fees, fraud, ACI, capture delay).\n\nA helper module is importable as `helper` (`from helper import *`). Prefer it over re-deriving the rules; its functions:\ndef load_payments() -> pd.DataFrame:  # The transactions table (payments.csv).\ndef load_fees() -> list[dict[str, Any]]:  # The 1000 fee rules (fees.json); null or [] in a field means the rule applies to all values.\ndef load_merchants() -> list[dict[str, Any]]:  # Merchant metadata (merchant_data.json): capture_delay, acquirer, MCC, account_type.\ndef load_acquirer_countries() -> pd.DataFrame:  # acquirer -> country_code (acquirer_countries.csv).\ndef load_mcc() -> pd.DataFrame:  # mcc -> description (merchant_category_codes.csv).\ndef get_merchant_info(name: str) -> dict[str, Any]:  # The merchant_data.json record for one merchant.\ndef calculate_fee(fixed_amount: float, rate: float, transaction_value: float) -> float:  # manual.md: fee = fixed_amount + rate * transaction_value / 10000.\ndef fraud_rate(df: pd.DataFrame) -> float:  # Fraction (0-1) of eur_amount volume in df that is fraudulent (manual.md section 7).\ndef fraud_rate_by(df: pd.DataFrame, group_col: str) -> pd.Series:  # Fraud rate (fraudulent volume / total volume) per value of group_col, sorted descending.\ndef day_of_year_to_month(day_of_year: int, year: int) -> int:  # Convert payments.csv's (year, day_of_year) into a calendar month (1-12).\ndef capture_delay_bucket(merchant_capture_delay: str) -> str:  # Map a merchant_data.json capture_delay ('immediate', 'manual', or a day count\ndef fraud_level_bucket(fraud_rate_pct: float) -> str:  # Map a fraud rate in PERCENT (e.g. 8.0 for 8%) onto fees.json's monthly_fraud_level bucket.\ndef volume_bucket(monthly_volume_eur: float) -> str:  # Map a monthly EUR volume onto fees.json's monthly_volume bucket.\ndef monthly_merchant_stats(payments: pd.DataFrame, merchant: str, year: int, month: int) -> dict[str, Any]:  # Natural-month stats for one merchant, per manual.md section 5 (\"Monthly volumes\ndef is_intracountry(issuing_country: str, acquirer_country: str) -> bool:  # manual.md section 5: intracountry (domestic) means issuer country == acquirer\ndef _field_matches(rule_value: Any, actual_value: Any) -> bool:\ndef fee_rule_matches(\ndef applicable_fee_ids(\ndef transactions_matching_rule(\ndef fee_total_for_rule(\ndef total_fees_paid(\ndef average_fee_across_rules(fees: list[dict[str, Any]], transaction_value: float, **filters: Any) -> float:  # Average fee, at the given transaction_value, across every fee rule matching\n\nTool output longer than 1500 characters is cut to its first 600 characters plus a reference like [out#3 …]. To read more of it call show('out#3') for the next window, show('out#3', start=1500) for a later one, or show('out#3', find='text') to jump to a match. Print only what you need.\n\nQUESTION: What is the fee ID or IDs that apply to account_type = R and aci = B?\n\nGUIDELINES: Answer must be a list of values in comma separated list, eg: A, B, C. If the answer is an empty list, reply with an empty string. If a question does not have a relevant or applicable answer for the task, please respond with 'Not Applicable'"
